*main/
*master/
190/

# `*main/` / `*master/` exist to keep the vendored source dumps (docs.rs-main,
# phira-docs-main, d3d9-to-11-master, ...) out of the repo. `phira-main` is vendored too, but it
# is ALREADY COMMITTED - 681 files - and `prpr-port`, the crate we wrote, lives inside it.
#
# A directory pattern excludes the directory *itself*, and git cannot re-include a file whose
# parent directory is excluded. So without the negations below, every new file under `phira-main`
# needed `git add -f` and nothing said so: `prpr-port/src/host.rs` and
# `prpr-port/src/bin/host_m1.rs` were silently invisible to `git status` until this was noticed.
# Un-ignoring the tree also has the side benefit that an edit to a vendored source becomes a
# visible diff instead of an un-diffable one.
!phira-main/

# Matches the outer and the inner `phira-main` directory; both end in "main".
# Everything else under those (prpr, mpr, docs, ...) is tracked or will now show up as ??.

# Build output, not source.
phira-main/**/target/

# Same for the port's own crates. `rust-wp81/shims/{macroquad,miniquad}/.gitignore` already say
# `target/`, but those files are untracked and the directories were committed before they existed,
# so a rule here is what actually keeps them out.
#
# Listed one crate at a time rather than as `rust-wp81/**/target/`, because that pattern also
# matches `rust-wp81/work/library/vendor/cc-1.4.3/src/target/` - which is *source* (apple.rs,
# llvm.rs, parser.rs, ...), not build output. A new crate needs a line here.
rust-wp81/compat/target/
rust-wp81/d3d11-rs-probe/target/
rust-wp81/link-probe/target/
rust-wp81/shims/macroquad/target/
rust-wp81/shims/miniquad/target/
rust-wp81/shims/rfd/target/
rust-wp81/shims/sasa/target/

# --- C# build output and packaging output -----------------------------------
# MSBuild writes `bin`/`obj` beside every project and VS writes `AppPackages`
# beside the packaged ones.  All three were committed - 163 files at the phone
# project alone, 653 MB of `.appx`/`.appxupload` across history - which is why
# the ignore rule that already existed in `Phirura.WindowsPhone/.gitignore`
# never appeared to do anything: `.gitignore` has no effect on a file git is
# already tracking.  They have now been dropped from the index *and* from
# history; these rules are what keep them out from here on.
#
# Written out per project rather than as a `Phirura/**/(bin|obj|AppPackages)`
# glob so it is obvious which project each line belongs to.  `Phirura.Windows`
# has no `.gitignore` of its own, so it needs these; the phone project's own
# file repeats them.
Phirura/Phirura/Phirura.Windows/bin/
Phirura/Phirura/Phirura.Windows/obj/
Phirura/Phirura/Phirura.Windows/AppPackages/

# Object files.  Two `wit-bindgen` `.o` were tracked and 611 `.o` blobs were in
# history; every one of them a cargo/MSVC intermediate, none of it source.
*.o

# NOT ignored: `Phirura/packages/Win2D.win81.1.21.0/bin/` is the NuGet package's
# own payload (Microsoft.Graphics.Canvas.*), not output of this solution.
# Dropping it would mean an offline build cannot link, so it stays tracked.
# Same for `rust-wp81/work/library/vendor/*/src/{bin,target}/` - those names are
# source (`hostcheck.rs`, `addr2line.rs`, `cc`'s `apple.rs`), not output.

# Written by rust-wp81/build-m1.ps1 next to the script.
*.log

prpr-macroquad/
prpr-miniquad/
trash/